---
import Research from '../../layouts/Research.astro';
import DeepZoom from '../../components/DeepZoom.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
---
<Research theme="immunohistochemistry" color="var(--blue)" eyebrow="Research · 02" title="Immunohistochemistry"
  lead="Making molecules visible: mapping plaques, cells and proteins behind the activity we record.">
  <Fragment slot="question">
    <h2>Where is β-amyloid in the brain, and how does pathology relate to the activity we record?</h2>
    <p>Staining brain sections lets us see when and where amyloid plaques appear — and compare young brains, where hyperactivity already exists without plaques, with older brains full of plaques.</p>
    <p class="small muted">Draft based on Zott et al. (2024) — please review wording.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="method">
    <h2>Antibodies as molecular probes</h2>
    <p>In immunohistochemistry, thin brain sections are incubated with antibodies that bind specific proteins. Fluorescent labels on these antibodies reveal where each protein sits — distinguishing, for example, neurons, astrocytes, interneuron subtypes or synaptic markers in the same section.</p>
    <p class="muted">Combining several labels (channels) in one section shows how cell types are arranged relative to each other — and links back to the cells imaged in vivo.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="data">
    <h2 data-reveal>Before and after plaques</h2>
    <p class="muted" data-reveal>Confocal images of coronal brain slices from APP23xPS45 mice. Switch between a young and an old animal, zoom in, and follow the numbered markers. Cyan: amyloid plaques (Methoxy-X04); grey: neurons (NeuroTrace).</p>
    <div style="margin-top:var(--s4)"><DeepZoom /></div>
    <p class="muted" style="margin-top:var(--s5)">To compare these sections with reference anatomy, see the <a href={url('/#methods')}>Allen Mouse Brain Atlas walkthrough on our homepage</a>, where you can move through the hippocampus section by section.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="findings">
    <ul class="findings">
      <li><strong>Plaques appear with age.</strong> At 2.5 months APP23xPS45 brains are essentially plaque-free; by 8 months, Methoxy-X04-positive plaques fill cortex and hippocampus, including the CA1 region we image.</li>
      <li><strong>Early is different from late.</strong> Treatment that prevented hyperactivity before plaque formation had no effect once plaques were present. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-50153-y">Zott et al., Nat Commun 2024</a></li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</Research>
<style>.findings { display: grid; gap: var(--s3); padding-left: 1.1em; } .findings li { max-width: var(--measure); }</style>
